List progress notes for the current account. Customers commonly call these progress notes, but also shift notes, client notes, or communications — use this tool for any of those. Results are scoped to the notes the requesting user is permitted to see: with data access policies enforced, the list may be filtered (e.g. team-based visibility) or empty even when matching notes exist in the account, so an empty result does not mean there are no notes. Notes are categorised (enquiry, feedback, incident, injury, notes, mileage) and can be filtered by client, staff author, shift, category, and date range. created_from is required and bounds how far back by note creation date the search goes (it keeps queries off the full history of a large, time-partitioned table). Set it to match the span the request implies (anywhere up to 90 days); when the user gives no start date at all, default to 7 days ago. created_from must never be more than 90 days ago — a value older than 90 days is rejected. This means the tool only returns notes that were CREATED in the last 90 days. shift_date_from / shift_date_to (and shift_id) filter by the shift's date, not the note's creation date, so they narrow which recently created notes you get (and are cheaper, via the shifts(start_at) index) — but they cannot surface notes that were written more than 90 days ago. If a search returns nothing, the notes may simply have been created outside this 90-day window rather than not existing. Returns up to 20 notes per page, defaulting to most recent first (created_at desc); change ordering with sort_by / sort_type. time_zone is required: every date and timestamp in the response (created_at, updated_at, shift_date) is rendered in that one zone, so the whole response is internally consistent.

