Update an existing shift by id. This edits ONE shift. If the shift is part of a recurring series (`recurring` is true on the shift) and the user means the change to apply from here on — "move the Tuesday visit to 10am from now on" — use `update_shift_recurrence_details` instead, which applies field values across every occurrence up to a date you give it. Changing the series' schedule itself — how often it repeats, which days it falls on, when it ends — is `update_shift_recurrence_pattern`, if that tool is in your tool list; it rebuilds the series so every shift id changes, so never reach for it to change a field value. Where it is not available to you, changing a schedule is not possible here at all: say so and direct the user to the web UI. Partial update — send `id` plus only the fields you want to change; omitted fields are left untouched. Use `list_shifts` to find the shift id. Use `client_ids` / `staff_ids` to reassign clients/staff by id (look them up via `list_clients` / `list_staff`); sending either REPLACES all existing assignments wholesale, so include every id that should remain. Price-book, fund, pay-group, and per-shift travel-billing selection are not supported via MCP; this tool leaves each client's travel billing on the shift exactly as it is, whether that is the client's own Invoice Travel setting or a per-shift override set elsewhere. Additional charges (transport, equipment, and similar extras billed to a client on the shift) are not supported via MCP either — do not attempt to add, change, or remove them with this tool; direct the user to the web UI instead. State rules: invoiced shifts cannot be updated (409); cancelled shifts cannot be edited here — use the cancel tool. Requires the v3 shift update endpoint to be enabled for the account. When changing `start_at`/`end_at` or reassigning staff via `staff_ids`, check for conflicts first: for each affected staff member call `list_shifts` over a whole-day date range (from_date/to_date as YYYY-MM-DD) covering the shift's new date(s) and the day before — not the exact start/end times, because `list_shifts` filters on each shift's start_at, so a narrow window misses earlier-starting or overnight shifts that still overlap. Compare the returned shifts' start_at/end_at (excluding the shift being edited) against the new window; if any overlap, surface the conflicting shift and ask whether to proceed anyway or choose someone else rather than updating silently. Validate the time range before calling: if `end_at` is earlier than `start_at` on the same calendar day, do not call this tool yet and do not assume an overnight shift. First ask the user to confirm whether they meant an overnight shift (in which case set `end_at` to the next day with an explicit offset) or made a typo, and wait for their answer before updating the shift.

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__shiftcare_au__update_shift(args: {
  // AccountLocation ID. Use the `list_account_locations` tool to discover valid IDs for this account.
  account_location_id?: number;
  // Full address
  address?: string;
  // IDs of allowances to attach to the shift. Use the `list_allowances` tool to discover valid IDs.
  allowance_ids?: Array<number>;
  // Break duration in minutes, or 0 for no break. Any nonzero value is rejected with a 400 once break tracking is enabled for the account — per-worker break times are not yet available through this API.
